1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:01,617
Open the C08 template.

2
00:00:01,617 --> 00:00:06,338
It is deliberately short, the same design choice as C06 and C07: something you can

3
00:00:06,338 --> 00:00:11,060
actually fill in before you connect a credential, not a form substantial enough to

4
00:00:11,060 --> 00:00:12,730
skip when you are in a hurry.

5
00:00:12,730 --> 00:00:17,566
Keep it open while you build anything new for the rest of this course, and honestly,

6
00:00:17,566 --> 00:00:18,315
after it too.

7
00:00:18,315 --> 00:00:22,864
One scale note before the questions: the worksheet runs per capability, not per

8
00:00:22,864 --> 00:00:23,209
agent.

9
00:00:23,209 --> 00:00:27,873
An agent gaining two tools is two worksheets, because each tool has its own reach

10
00:00:27,873 --> 00:00:32,595
and its own narrower option, and bundling them into one form is how the smaller of

11
00:00:32,595 --> 00:00:37,143
the two rides through unexamined on the bigger one's paperwork, the way a minor

12
00:00:37,143 --> 00:00:41,347
clause rides through on a contract everyone read for its headline number.

13
00:00:41,897 --> 00:00:46,329
Question two is where most trouble would get caught if people actually asked it.

14
00:00:46,329 --> 00:00:50,540
"Wider" is not automatically wrong, but it means you are not adding to a job

15
00:00:50,540 --> 00:00:55,195
description anymore, you are writing a new one, and it deserves the same care as C05

16
00:00:55,195 --> 00:00:56,247
did the first time.

17
00:00:56,247 --> 00:01:00,680
The sign-off row's four checkboxes are the first four questions' receipts: scope

18
00:01:00,680 --> 00:01:05,002
named, permission actually checked, human step decided, blast radius answered.

19
00:01:05,002 --> 00:01:07,163
Nothing clever, and that is the design.

20
00:01:07,163 --> 00:01:10,099
The worksheet does not know anything you do not know.

21
00:01:10,099 --> 00:01:14,255
It just refuses to let any one of the four go unasked, and "all of them, no

22
00:01:14,255 --> 00:01:18,687
exceptions" exists because the box people skip is always the one that would have

23
00:01:18,687 --> 00:01:19,629
been interesting.

24
00:01:20,179 --> 00:01:21,590
Read that last line again.

25
00:01:21,590 --> 00:01:25,876
The worksheet, filled in honestly, would have caught the exact mistake the next

26
00:01:25,876 --> 00:01:27,883
lecture is about, before it happened.

27
00:01:27,883 --> 00:01:29,077
That is not hindsight.

28
00:01:29,077 --> 00:01:30,922
That is what the worksheet is for.

29
00:01:30,922 --> 00:01:35,316
And be precise about what "should stop you" means, because it is not abandonment.

30
00:01:35,316 --> 00:01:39,548
It means the connect step waits while you go and find the narrower option: the

31
00:01:39,548 --> 00:01:43,563
label-scoped grant, the restricted key, the folder instead of the account.

32
00:01:43,563 --> 00:01:48,120
Usually that search takes ten minutes and succeeds, and the addition proceeds with a

33
00:01:48,120 --> 00:01:49,639
radius that matches its job.

34
00:01:49,639 --> 00:01:54,142
Occasionally no narrower option exists, and then you are making a genuine decision,

35
00:01:54,142 --> 00:01:58,049
wide access or no capability, with your eyes open, on paper, in advance.

36
00:01:58,049 --> 00:02:01,304
Both outcomes beat the third one, which is the next lecture.

37
00:02:01,854 --> 00:02:06,194
Do the exercise with a real capability, not a hypothetical one, and do it even if

38
00:02:06,194 --> 00:02:08,659
you have no intention of adding anything soon.

39
00:02:08,659 --> 00:02:13,053
The worksheet's questions only become reflexes through your hands, and ten minutes

40
00:02:13,053 --> 00:02:17,447
with a capability you might genuinely want is the cheapest rehearsal you will ever

41
00:02:17,447 --> 00:02:20,020
get for the Thursday when you want one urgently.

42
00:02:20,020 --> 00:02:24,467
Next lecture is the story this worksheet exists to prevent, told in full, including

43
00:02:24,467 --> 00:02:26,771
the part where the guardrail actually held.
